/* The roster, composed onto ScreenScaffold. Cards do their own styling; this
 * is the grid around them, which is the one thing neither the frame nor
 * CreatureCard can own - a component's stylesheet reaches the component and
 * its descendants, never the container it was put in (StatCell.uss's closing
 * note has the case that found it).
 *
 * TWO RULES LEFT THIS FILE IN PHASE 8 TASK 10 AND NEITHER WAS REPLACED BY A
 * COPY HERE:
 *   - the root's `padding: var(--gutter)` is the scaffold's content region,
 *     which puts the gutter on `unity-scroll-view__content-container` so an
 *     elevated card's shadow is not clipped at the viewport edge.
 *   - `__notice`'s colour, size, wrap and margin are the scaffold's footer
 *     note, which also collapses when the sentence is empty. The old Label
 *     did not: a complete roster drew a blank 20px strip above the grid, and
 *     it is visible in every capture of this screen before this task.
 */
.roster-view { flex-grow: 1; }

/* THE ONE EYEBROW IN THE APP THAT CARRIES DECISION-BEARING NUMBERS, and the
 * one place a screen's own sheet has to reach into the scaffold's header.
 *
 * `HATCHERY · 14 OF 20 SLOTS` (`Creature Roster.dc.html:29`) is how full the
 * Hatchery is, which is what `RosterScreen.IsFull` answers and what decides
 * whether a claim can be taken at all - bible 10.6's own test for a figure
 * that needs the tabular face and the 11px floor. `RosterView`'s constructor
 * adds `t-num` to the label for the face; this rule is the floor, and it has
 * to be here because `.t-micro` is DECLARED AFTER `.t-num` in Theme.uss, so
 * at equal specificity the 10px wins and the marker alone would change the
 * font and not the size. That is the hole `verify-uss-tokens.sh`'s check 2
 * documents at its own foot: the gate reads for --text-micro under a
 * `.t-num` SELECTOR and cannot see a class that loses the cascade.
 *
 * TWO CLASSES, so this beats `.screen-scaffold__eyebrow`'s own colour rule
 * without depending on sheet order - the same specificity trick
 * `CostCtaRow.uss` uses for its three size overrides.
 *
 * 11 AGAINST THE HANDOFF'S 9. LineageStrip.uss already recorded this exact
 * trade for its 8.5px generation labels and made the same call: the floor
 * wins, and the 2px is written down rather than argued with silently. */
.roster-view .screen-scaffold__eyebrow { font-size: var(--text-secondary); }

/* Two 160px CreatureCards on a 430 frame with a 12px gutter: 406 of space for
 * 320 of card, so `space-between` is what puts the pair against both edges
 * and an odd third card at the left where the next row starts. */
.roster-view__cards { flex-direction: row; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; }

/* 198px, WHICH IS THE HANDOFF'S GRID AND NOT `CreatureCard`'s OWN WIDTH.
 * `Creature Roster.dc.html:54` is `grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 9px`
 * inside the 12px gutter - so on this project's own 406px content column
 * each tile is (406 - 9) / 2 = 198.5, rounded down. The component ships at a
 * fixed 160 because that is what it is in a horizontal strip (the reveal's
 * consumed pair, the post-wave drop), and 160 in a 406 column left 46px of
 * dead paper down the middle AND squeezed two trait chips onto two rows
 * where the handoff fits them on one. Both are visible in the first capture
 * of this screen.
 *
 * A SCREEN'S SHEET REACHING A COMPONENT IT COMPOSED is the legal direction
 * (StatCell.uss's closing note); `CreatureCard`'s own 160 does not move, and
 * the four other screens that place one are untouched. */
/* TWO CLASSES, AND THE FIRST CAPTURE OF THIS RULE IS WHY. `.creature-card`
 * sets `width: 160px` and is ALSO a single class, so at equal specificity the
 * winner is whichever sheet the panel applies last - and `CreatureCard.uss`
 * is attached inside the card's own subtree, which is deeper than this
 * screen's, so it applies later and wins. Written with one class the rule
 * compiled, passed both gates, and changed nothing: the capture came back
 * byte-identical to the one before it. A screen's sheet reaches a component
 * it composed, but it has to outrank the component's own rule to do it -
 * which is the same two-class move `CostCtaRow.uss` and `MutationBanner.uss`
 * make against `icons.uss` and say so. */
/* 48%, NOT 198px: two 198px cards fit the 430 capture frame and not the 402
 * phone the game ships to (the simulator walk of Phase 10 Task 1.8 showed one
 * column). A percentage is two columns at every width the corpus and the
 * device share. */
.roster-view__cards > .roster-view__card { width: 48%; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }

/* The empty case is one element in a row that lays out tiles, so it has to be
 * told to take the whole line - a CreatureCard is a fixed 160px and an
 * EmptyState has no width of its own. Without this, `space-between` would
 * park a centred sentence against the left edge at whatever width its text
 * happened to measure. */
.roster-view__cards > .empty-state { flex-grow: 1; }
